   (b) Torn content. MERGED: TestProbeR11_NoTornContent (8 writers x 2 MiB x
       20 rounds) "torn-rounds=0 of 20", PASS; and the oracle's own
       ConcurrentWritesNeverTear passes under -race in accept.sh step 5.
       PRE-MERGE, honest detail: post-hoc reads after all writers finish did
       NOT tear on this kernel/fs (torn-rounds=0 of 20; a wider hunt at 12
       writers x 24 MiB x 12 rounds also found 0) because Linux completes a
       multi-MiB write(2) to a regular file in one call. The tear IS reachable
       and I reached it from a reader's vantage instead:
       TestProbeR11_ReaderNeverSeesPartial against PRE-MERGE logged
       "complete-reads=7476 partial-or-failed-reads=1079" with repeated
       "reader saw length 0 want 2097152" — writer A's os.WriteFile truncates
       the shared staging file while writer B renames it onto the destination,
       so the destination momentarily holds a 0-length file. That is the
       torn/truncated-length consequence, observed. Against MERGED the same
       probe logs "complete-reads=204 partial-or-failed-reads=0", PASS.
   Both consequences are unreachable now, and structurally so: storage.go:449
   `os.CreateTemp(dir, filepath.Base(path)+".tmp-*")` gives each writer a
   private staging inode, so no writer can truncate or rename another's.

